  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Fair Finance Asia-Sida-Cividep</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Fair Finance Asia Program (FFA) envisages to reduce the negative impact of national and cross-border bank investments in Asia on human rights, the environment and climate change, and to increase adequate financial services for pro-poor, inclusive economic development. The goal is to establish a sustainable financial sector in Asia, with financial sector institutions operating at national and regional level being more transparent and accountable and adhering to Economic, Social and Governance (ESG)-criteria. FFA is funded by Sida.

This project is implemented by Cividep, who will lead on the establishment and execution of the Fair Finance Guide India during period August 2022 to December 2022. It envisages to contribute to FFA by undertaking research to understand the bank internal policies related to labour and environment, their way of reporting of their lending and investment policies, and the patterns of (un-)disclosure of good policies. Based on the research conducted, advocacy efforts are devised to transparently engage with RBI and Ministry of Finance by supporting institutions like IBA and Unions who are speaking the language of responsible banking. General awareness is built amongst the wider public on responsible banking and amongst at least 8 banks to be more conscious of the opportunity they have, through responsible policy-making and disclosures, to help protect people, especially the most excluded, and the environment.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Based in Bangalore, and with field-offices in other locations in South India, Cividep India&#x2019;s work aims to safeguard the rights of communities, especially workers employed in global supply chains. Cividep India focuses on a selection of India&#x2019;s most significant export-oriented industries, striving to hold corporate entities accountable for the impacts of their business on workers and the environment.

Cividep works in the area of business and human rights, and engages directly with a range of India&#x2019;s export sectors: garment, leather, and electronics industries, coffee and tea plantations. Cividep India considers corporate accountability to be the mostfundamental aspect of responsible business conduct. The concept recognises that corporations have ethical, moral and legal obligations to their employees and workers, and to communities directly impacted by corporate activities.

Cividep India collaborate with national and international organizations and networks for the advancement of responsible business conduct and human rights, and with partners both in India and abroad, in pursuit of decent work for all.</narrative>
